 ## Background
-The application of machine learning to volume rendering classification emerged in the early 2000s as a response to the complexity of high-dimensional data. Tzeng et al. introduced intelligent systems using Support Vector Machines (SVMs) and neural networks for higher-dimensional classification, demonstrating the reuse of classifiers across similar datasets [S1]. Concurrently, Takanashi et al. proposed using Independent Component Analysis (ICA) to simplify the classification space for multidimensional volumes [S3, unofficial]. More recently, self-supervised vision transformers (e.g., DINO) have been leveraged for segmentation-based transfer function design, although computational costs remain a challenge [S2].
